data.content:
The `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` 10.0 package upgraded from **OpenAPI.NET v1 to v2** (`Microsoft.OpenApi 2.x`). This is a major breaking change in the underlying library. Here's what changed and how to fix each piece:

---

## 1. `OpenApiString` / `OpenApiAny` removed

OpenAPI.NET v2 removed the `Microsoft.OpenApi.Any` namespace. Schema examples are now raw `JsonNode`.

```csharp
// Before
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Any;
param.Schema.Example = new OpenApiString("1.0");

// After
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
```

---

## 2. `OpenApiResponse` → responses are now `IDictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>`

The `Responses` property type changed from `OpenApiResponses` (which inherited `Dictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>`) to a plain dictionary. The `TryAdd` call still works, but you may need to adjust if you were using `OpenApiResponses`-specific members. Your existing `TryAdd` line should compile as-is.

---

## 3. `OpenApiReference.Type` → `OpenApiReference.Id` only

References no longer use `Type` + `Id`. Instead, use the `ReferenceId` on the scheme directly or construct a reference via the new model:

```csharp
// Before
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "oauth2" }
};

// After
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Id = "oauth2", Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme }
};
```

Actually this part is mostly unchanged syntactically — but `ReferenceType` moved to `Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References`. If you get a compile error here, add:

```csharp
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References;
```

Or use the new shorthand class:

```csharp
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2");
```

---

## 4. `OpenApiSecurityRequirement` is no longer a `Dictionary`

It changed from inheriting `Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>` to having a `Schemes` property:

```csharp
// Before
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new() { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
};

// After
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new() { Schemes = { [oAuthScheme] = scopes } }
};
```

---

## 5. `schema.Nullable` removed

OpenAPI v3.1 (which v2 of the library targets) dropped `nullable` in favor of a `type` array. Use the `Type` flags:

```csharp
// Before
property.Value.Nullable = false;

// After — to make NOT nullable, ensure "null" isn't in the type
property.Value.Type = property.Value.Type & ~JsonSchemaType.Null;
```

`JsonSchemaType` is a flags enum (`String`, `Number`, `Null`, etc.):

```csharp
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;
```

---

## Summary of required `using` changes

```csharp
// Remove
// using Microsoft.OpenApi.Any;

// Add
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;           // JsonSchemaType, etc.
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References; // if using reference classes
```

These changes reflect the library's shift to OpenAPI v3.1 semantics and native `System.Text.Json` types replacing the custom `IOpenApiAny` hierarchy.
